A||||2||ChadMan||110-hp Iron Duke
Z||000000||ChadMan||05-08-99||07:00 AM||||Over the years, I've gotten pretty familiar with the original 92-hp Iron Duke 4-banger (84-86) and the 98-hp Iron Duke (87-88).  However, a friend told me that the 89-94 Iron Duke made more power.  My dad had a 1991 Oldsmobile brochure lying around (don't ask) and sure enough, they claim 110 hp at 5200 rpm for the 2.5 4-cyl in both the Cutlass Calais and Cutlass Ciera. I then went to the library and consulted a Chilton's manual for Cieras and it verified the 110hp figure for 89-94.<p>My questions:  Does anybody know what the mods were that resulted in more hp and a higher redline?  If it's just a reworked head and/or injection system, could this be retrofitted to earlier Dukes?  And couldn't this engine (with its ECM) be transplanted into a Fiero to replace a worn-out Iron Duke?
Z||000001||theogre||05-08-99||12:31 PM||||all 4 cyls are interchangeable, but you need to switch some pullys and stuff.<p>to install a later year, you will, yes, need the ecm, and probably allot of the wireing. The ECM design changed in 87.
Z||000002||Hock||05-25-99||11:15 AM||||the iron duke was produced for many years.  but, unfortitly it's last year was 1984.  after that GM went to a differant kind of block.  the engine you are looking at is a U code engine. the fiero has an R code.  the major parts from these engine are not interchangeable.  they are two seperate engines. the only parts that are the same is the rods and pistons ( not sure if the intake or the exhaust man. are the same).  yes they do have more power than the R code.  as far as putting that engine in a fiero it should be possible, BUT, i would research the swap before i did it. 
